WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.012 --> 00:00:02.220
SPEAKER 1: While big data
brings us valuable insights

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:05.100
and opportunities, it also
brings the responsibility

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.200
to ensure that data
is secure, meaning

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.080
that only the right data is
shared with the right people.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.150
In today's episode of
"BigQuery Spotlight"

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:14.490
we're talking about how to
use Google Cloud's Identity

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:16.500
and Access Management
Service to define

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.780
which users can query
in your projects

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:20.340
and access your data sets.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:22.755
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:27.102 --> 00:00:29.727
BigQuery's a fully
managed service.

00:00:29.727 --> 00:00:32.060
That means it takes advantage
of Google's infrastructure

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:33.140
security.

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:34.940
Google secures its
infrastructure end

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:37.970
to end, from physical
security of its services

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:40.430
to stringent
operational practices.

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:42.170
But today's episode
is about the role

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:45.020
you play in keeping your
projects and data secure.

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:47.100
Specifically, how you
share project and data

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.310
set access with different
end users and groups

00:00:49.310 --> 00:00:50.630
in your company.

00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:53.090
The key to managing access
to your projects and data

00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:57.110
is the Identity and Access
Management Service or IAM.

00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:59.630
Cloud IAM allows you to
manage access control

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:03.740
by defining three things;
member, role and policy.

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:08.190
Meaning you specify who has
what access to which resource.

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:10.100
Let's take a closer
look at each of these.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:13.070
First, the member,
or who has access.

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:16.140
You can define a
member in several ways.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:18.860
One way is to identify end
users by their email address

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:21.390
if it's associated
with a Google account.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:23.570
You can also set access
for a group of users

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:27.140
by using a Google Group, G
Suite domain, or Cloud Identity

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:28.940
domain as the member.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:31.820
Sometimes it's not a person,
but an application or service

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:34.100
that needs access to
your BigQuery data.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:36.720
In this case, you can
create a service account,

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:38.930
which is a special type
of Google account intended

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:41.390
to represent a non-human user.

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:43.160
When you create
service account, it

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:45.050
is assigned a
special address which

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:48.830
can be used as the member's
identity when defining access.

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:52.240
When I give a data set
access to any logged user,

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:53.900
there is a special
identifier called,

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:55.880
all authenticated
users, which you can

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:58.080
use as the member in this case.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.980
This is exactly how we make
our BigQuery public data sets

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:02.990
available to all BigQuery users.

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:04.610
Once you have
defined the member,

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:07.130
you need to decide what
role to give that member.

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:08.810
A role includes a
set of permissions

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:10.759
that determines which
operations the member

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:12.230
is allowed to perform.

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:14.870
Cloud IAM provides
several predefined roles

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:17.870
that include a combination
of these permissions.

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:19.400
They include things
like the ability

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.950
to view or edit data
sets, and the ability

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:23.540
to run queries and
store data which

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:24.930
are billed to the project.

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:27.860
It is also possible to
create custom roles.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:30.770
These allow you to bundle one
or more permissions into a role

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:33.140
to meet your specific needs.

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:35.660
You will have to manage your
custom role as new features,

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:38.900
permissions and services
are added to Google Cloud.

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:42.710
So for this reason, we recommend
sticking to predefined roles.

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:45.260
You can always assign more
than one predefined role

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:46.430
to a member.

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:48.440
Together, the
member and the role

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:50.480
make up the policy,
which is then

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:52.700
applied to a specific resource.

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:55.180
The policy could be attached
to a specific table,

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:58.340
data set, or the
GCP project itself.

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:00.140
At the project
level, permissions

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:02.630
apply actual current
and future data sets

00:03:02.630 --> 00:03:04.560
that are part of the project.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.040
Let's see how it works
in a specific scenario.

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:09.530
Suppose you're a retail
company with two groups of data

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:10.460
analysts.

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:13.040
The first group analyzes
your retail sales,

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:16.100
and the second analyzes
customer service requests.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:18.920
These two types of data are
stored in separate data sets

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:20.720
within your project.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:23.690
Both groups of analysts need
full access, read and write,

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:25.610
to the data set
that they work on

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:28.370
and access to run
queries on the project.

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:31.310
Let's head to the console
and set up the policies.

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:33.950
You'll start by giving the
sales analysts the BigQuery data

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:36.620
editor role for the
retail sales data set.

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:38.420
To set roles at
the data set level,

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:40.640
select the data
set from resources,

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:42.890
then click share data set
near the right-hand side

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:44.580
of the window.

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:46.710
On the data set
permissions tab, enter

00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:50.030
the entity you want to add
into the Add Members text box.

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:53.130
For select a role, select
BigQuery data editor and click

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:53.880
Add.

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:55.680
Then click done.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:58.410
Then follow the same steps
to give the customer service

00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:00.960
analysts the BigQuery data
editor role for the customer

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:02.820
service data set.

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:05.700
You'll notice we're using
Google groups to grant roles.

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:07.140
Google groups are
a convenient way

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:09.660
to create a policy for
collection of users.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:12.270
You can grant and change access
for a whole group at once,

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:15.070
instead of individual
users one at a time.

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:17.519
Then you can easily add
or remove members directly

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:19.560
from the Google Group itself.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:22.050
Giving the analyst the data
editor role at the data set

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:24.420
level gives them the ability
to access all the tables

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:27.103
within that particular
data set, but it does not

00:04:27.103 --> 00:04:28.770
give them the permission
to actually run

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:31.290
queries billed to that project.

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:33.870
For that, they must be granted
the project level role called,

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:35.670
BigQuery user.

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:37.620
To grant roles at
the project level,

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:39.840
you'll need to head to
the navigation window.

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:44.280
Hover over IAM in admin and
then click to select IAM.

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:46.770
At the top of the
screen, click add.

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:48.810
In the new members
box, enter both email

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:51.690
addresses to represent
your analyst groups.

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:54.990
Select the BigQuery user
role and click Save.

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:58.200
Now your two teams are ready and
equipped to analyze their data

00:04:58.200 --> 00:04:59.162
securely.

00:04:59.162 --> 00:05:00.870
If you want to learn
more about BigQuery,

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:02.530
check out the rest
of our playlist.

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:04.363
And don't forget to
subscribe to our channel

00:05:04.363 --> 00:05:06.162
on YouTube for
other great content.

00:05:06.162 --> 00:05:08.370
Look out for our next episode
of "BigQuery Spotlight"

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:09.930
and remember, stay curious.

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:12.680
[MUSIC PLAYING]

